WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
There aren't many pneumatic
tube systems left in the world.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.460
They used to be...
well, not everywhere,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.920
but a hundred years ago,
most major western cities

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.840
would have a few of them for
sending messages around,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.060
either within a building
or between buildings.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.040
At one point, New York had

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.280
27 miles of pneumatic tubes
between post offices.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.960
And you can still
see them sometimes,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.700
in American
drive-through banks,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.100
or in a few hospitals for
quickly moving samples around.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.500
Last year, I visited one
in Canada that's used for

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.840
transmitting small amounts
of radioactive stuff

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:28.920
for medical therapy.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.680
I know of only one place,
however,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.420
that sends food through them.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.000
This is C1 Espresso in
Christchurch, New Zealand,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.140
and about 10 years ago,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.620
they installed a brand new
set of pneumatic tubes.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.020
- Customer places an order
at the counter,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.120
which will then get sent
through to the kitchen.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.820
From there, the chefs
can open it up,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.140
see what they need to make,

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.840
if it's a meal that goes
inside a pneumatic tube,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.800
we've got a map on the wall,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.320
our chefs will then know
where that customer is sitting

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.060
and which tube to send it to.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.860
We're a wee bit limited to
what we can put through them.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.240
Soup's never a good option.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.540
Burgers, fries will be
packaged in these tubes,

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.920
stainless steel, food grade.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.560
We do throw a can of beer
through them every now and then,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.900
so long as people don't mind
getting a bit fizzed up

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:10.860
when it arrives at their table.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.160
But otherwise, we get our
bakers to specially make

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.780
a torpedo-shaped burger.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.440
Most companies don't have
fancy see-through tubes.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.420
They were made in Germany
and imported in,

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.080
especially for us.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.020
- As restaurant gimmicks go,
I really like this.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.280
There are a few competitors

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.720
out there in the world
that use things like

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.600
conveyor belts or miniature
rollercoaster tracks,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.080
but here, your food isn't open
to cold air as it travels.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.360
But I've been here filming
for an hour or two now,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.840
and most of the people here
are not ordering the menu items

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.700
that go through
the pneumatic tubes.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.220
They're ordering the rest of
the menu, or the coffees.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.060
These tubes attract the tourists,
but the repeat customers are locals,

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.700
and they're here for actually
good coffee and food.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.780
Turns out that a cafe like this
can't survive without

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.040
actually being good.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.320
- We've been around for 11 years now.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.380
We have a roastery where we roast
our own coffee in-house

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.020
and create our own blend,
called C1000.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.840
The pneumatic tubes were
set up from day one.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.340
The main issues we have
around maintenance

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.920
is the actual tubes themselves,
wear and tear on those.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.008
- There we go.

00:02:13.008 --> 00:02:13.924
Oops.

00:02:14.908 --> 00:02:16.020
Five second rule.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.720
- They create friction and
get stuck within the system.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.320
Everything else, touch wood,
seems to work relatively well...

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:23.460
...most of the time.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.620
We're really lucky, the new
Christchurch Hospital,

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.300
about a kilometer
down the road,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.100
still uses a
pneumatic tube system.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.740
There's a company in Auckland
that's able to come down

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.200
and do repairs and maintenance
on ours when they're down

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.060
looking after the
hospital as well.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.680
One of the main issues we have,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.380
especially in a busy kitchen
when we've got a full cafe,

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.480
tube doesn't get closed properly,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.420
decides with the vibrations
to wiggle itself open,

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.840
chips go everywhere and then
the tube actually stops

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.400
in the roof of the
middle of the cafe.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:51.840
If it's not too stuck,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.340
we'll try and push some
more air through

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.020
to see if it'll
unjam itself.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.680
Second option being,
we've got a unit that

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.480
sucks it back
the way it came.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.420
Third option, which is my
least favorite option,

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.520
is we have to cut them
out of the ceiling

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.240
at the end of the day.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.700
You hope it's near a join
that's already been cut.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.880
Maybe happens once
or twice a year.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.400
...fingers crossed.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.220
- Of course, I did ask if
I could put my GoPro camera

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.780
through the pneumatic tubes

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.580
to get pictures of
the food's journey.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.260
And they said yes!

